{"id":"ltc-disclosure-where-profit-went","title":"Where a year's profit went: into the amount owed by related companies","question":"When these homes made money in 2025, did the cash stay in the home or move to companies under the same owner?","finding":"The 42 homes reported $177.7 million of net income for their 2025 report year; the amount owed to them by related parties rose by $305.2 million. Most of the rise is at the 4 Life Generations Healthcare homes (Cedar Crest, Plum Tree, Pleasanton, Vista Manor): their related balance went from $32.9 million to $179.2 million in one year, on $15.3 million of net income, while the cash in their own accounts fell from $137.6 million to $816,962. At the other homes the rise ($159.0 million) is close to their profit; in 35 homes it was at least three quarters of the year's profit. Owners' draws, dividends and related-party transfers on the equity page are blank in every report.","how":"Rise = amount owed by related parties at the end of the report year less the same figure at the end of the year before (the 'prior period' column of the same report, pages 5.1 lines 50 and 160 and 5.2 lines 45 and 110; each prior figure was checked against the home's 2024 report in the State's complete data set and agrees). Cash = page 5.1 line 5. Net income = page 8 line 255; draws, dividends and related-party transfers = page 7 lines 11, 15 and 19. Group = the parent named on page 3.1, else the home-office party on page 10.4.","limits":"Homes in a group commonly send their cash to the group's central account and book the balance as owed to them (cash pooling); a one-year jump can come from moving to such an arrangement, from refinancing or from a sale inside the group. The Life Generations homes book the balance as a negative payable to related parties, which the case counts as owed to them. A balance owed remains the home's asset and can be repaid. The 2025 reports are as submitted and not yet audited.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"ho